// QR codes: byte mode, versions 1 to 40, all four error levels.
//
// The parts worth knowing about, because each is a place an implementation
// quietly goes wrong:
//
//   - **Reed-Solomon over GF(256)**, which is what makes a code readable with
//     a corner torn off. The error level chooses how much of the symbol is
//     redundancy: L recovers about 7% damage, H about 30%, and H at the same
//     data length needs a bigger symbol.
//   - **Interleaving.** Data is split into blocks, each gets its own error
//     words, and then the blocks are *interleaved* — codeword 0 of block 0,
//     codeword 0 of block 1, and so on. That is what turns a single physical
//     smudge into one lost codeword per block rather than a whole block lost.
//   - **Masking.** The eight masks exist because a symbol full of one colour,
//     or one containing something that looks like a finder pattern, is hard
//     to read. All eight are applied, each is scored by four penalty rules
//     from the standard, and the lowest wins. An encoder that picks a mask
//     without scoring produces valid symbols that some scanners refuse.
//   - **Format information appears twice**, in two places, BCH-encoded, so a
//     scanner can still find the error level and mask when one copy is
//     damaged. It is the one part of the symbol that is not itself protected
//     by Reed-Solomon.
//
// The tables in `tables.gr` are generated from the standard rather than
// typed; see `scripts/qr_tables.py`.

// <<< generated by scripts/qr_tables.py — do not edit below >>>

/// Five numbers per (version, level), in L, M, Q, H order: how many blocks of
